  "response": "**Setup (Bayes' theorem):**\n\n- Prevalence: P(D) = 1/500 = 0.002, P(¬D) = 0.998\n- Sensitivity: P(+|D) = 0.98\n- Specificity: P(–|¬D) = 0.95 → False positive rate: P(+|¬D) = 0.05\n\n**Compute P(+):**\n\nP(+) = P(+|D)·P(D) + P(+|¬D)·P(¬D)\nP(+) = (0.98 × 0.002) + (0.05 × 0.998)\nP(+) = 0.00196 + 0.0499\nP(+) = 0.05186\n\n**Apply Bayes' theorem:**\n\nP(D|+) = [P(+|D)·P(D)] / P(+) = 0.00196 / 0.05186 ≈ 0.0377941\n\n**As a percentage:** ≈ 3.78%\n\nThis illustrates the classic base-rate effect: even with a fairly sensitive/specific test, because the condition is rare (0.2% prevalence), most positive results are false positives — only about 1 in 26 people who test positive actually have the condition.\n\nFINAL ANSWER: 3.78",
